      {
        "identifier": "https://github.com/google-deepmind/formal-conjectures/blob/7bdc4a42f24c0316f81c73b26121794b2bed96a3/FormalConjectures/Arxiv/math.0608009/DixmierConjecture.lean",
        "note": "Supplies the four statements and the definition DixmierConjectureFor, and through its imports WeylAlgebra (FormalConjecturesForMathlib/Algebra/WeylAlgebra.lean), JacobianConjectureProp (FormalConjectures/Wikipedia/JacobianConjecture.lean) and RegularFunction with its Jacobian, comp and id (FormalConjecturesForMathlib/Algebra/MvPolynomial/RegularFunction.lean). Challenge.lean and DixJac/Defs.lean copy them verbatim, with their names; scripts/check-fc-match.py checks this against Formal Conjectures at that commit. The counterexample cG in DixJac/Main.lean is Formal Conjectures' JacobianConjecture.G with X 0, X 1, X 2 for X, Y, Z. Palomar does not accept escaped name components such as «math.0608009» in theorem names, so Challenge.lean states the theorems in the namespace DixmierConjecture, with Formal Conjectures' leaf names; Solution proves them under both names. Formal Conjectures itself already proves that the Jacobian conjecture fails in some dimension (JacobianConjecture.jacobian_conjecture, from G); this repository proves it in every dimension n >= 3, which the Dixmier statements need.",
